# How many assertions the SQL suite is known to execute.
#
# ONE definition, read by BOTH runners:
#
#   * `supabase/tests/run_locally.sh`, which globs `supabase/tests/*.sql`
#     against a throwaway cluster built with initdb, with `auth` and
#     `storage` stubbed;
#   * the "Run the database assertions" step in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`,
#     which runs a hand-kept list of the same 383 files against a real
#     Supabase local stack.
#
# It lived in `run_locally.sh` alone until this file existed, and CI --
# which is the authoritative runner -- counted nothing at all. So the
# floor guarded only the machine of whoever remembered to run the script,
# which is to say it guarded nothing. Two copies of the number would have
# drifted the first time one was raised; `scripts/check_assertion_floor.py`
# asserts that neither runner hardcodes one.
#
# WHAT THE NUMBER IS: the count of `NOTICE:  ok ` lines psql prints. Every
# `pg_temp.check_*` helper emits one on success, as do the handful of
# places that write `raise notice 'ok   ...'` by hand. It counts
# assertions that RAN, which is the point: 383 files passing is a weaker
# claim than 14,330 assertions holding, and a fixture that moves so a
# branch is never entered leaves every file green over fewer checks.
#
# HOW TO CHANGE IT: raise it when the number goes up, which is ordinary.
# Lower it only on purpose, in a commit that says why. A DIP with every
# file still green is the thing to investigate, not to paper over -- and
# if the cause is a branch that depends on today's date, that is a defect
# in the test rather than a reason to lower this.
#
# The bare integer below is the value. Anything else in this file is a
# comment; both readers anchor on `^[0-9]+$`.
#
# 5 October: 14330 -> 14332. Two assertions added to
# payroll_engine_sweep.sql, because a mutation run proved nothing
# asserted that `app.age_at` treats a MISSING date of birth as thirty --
# and that number decides an EPF rate, since `app.epf_category` flips at
# 60. RAISED IN THE SAME COMMIT AS THE ASSERTIONS, and from the measured
# figure (`all SQL assertions passed (383 files, 14332 assertions
# executed)`) rather than from 14330 + 2, because a floor that trails the
# count is a floor with room in it.
#
# 5 October, again: 14332 -> 14335. Three assertions added to
# payroll_run.sql, because a mutation run found that swapping the EPF and
# SOCSO employer EXPENSE accounts in `post_payroll_run` keeps the journal
# balanced and survived four test files. Measured, as above:
# `all SQL assertions passed (383 files, 14335 assertions executed)`.
#
# 5 October, third time: 14335 -> 14337. Two assertions added to
# control_accounts.sql, because adding 'debit_note' to the credit-note
# sign list in `app.post_sales_document_internal` -- a one-word edit
# that REVERSES the journal -- survived twelve test files. Measured:
# `all SQL assertions passed (383 files, 14337 assertions executed)`.
#
# 5 October, fourth time: 14337 -> 14341. Four assertions added to
# money_names_the_account.sql for three gaps in
# `app.post_receipt_internal` -- the default-account tiebreak, the
# oldest-active tiebreak, and the repost guard. Measured:
# `all SQL assertions passed (383 files, 14341 assertions executed)`.
#
# 14355, +14: `one_default_per_company.sql`, the 384th file. 0741 gives
# eight tables the partial unique index 0092 gave contact_addresses in
# 2024, so "the default" stops being whichever row came back first.
# Fourteen assertions in three groups, because an index can be wrong in
# three directions: absent, scoped globally so the second company gets
# none, and tightened past `and is_active` so a closed account may not
# hold a stale default. The first version of 0741 was wrong in the last
# two; neither was caught by these fourteen, both by the full suite
# (`pos_fnb.sql` and `money_names_the_account.sql`). Measured:
# `all SQL assertions passed (384 files, 14355 assertions executed)`.
#
# 14360, +5: 0742's section in the same file. A CHECK that a warehouse or
# pipeline cannot be default while inactive, so `where is_default` and
# `where is_default and is_active` select the same rows and the thirteen
# readers that omit `is_active` become correct without one of them being
